# ---- POINTER: HELD AT dbus-rel PENDING A ramabs/artistctl SINK FIX ----
# The absolute pointer for this station is BUILT AND PROVEN AT THE DEVICE
# LEVEL but is NOT cut over. On 2026-08-30 rhapsody was cut over to
# SH_INPUT_BACKEND=ramabs and its FIRST browser session drove the whole
# path correctly -- the guest's own coordinate read back the commanded
# pixel exactly -- but EVERY SESSION AFTER THE FIRST timed out
# negotiating the tile stream. 40 SESSION_ACCEPTED, zero completed, with
# the router counters frozen at the first session's numbers: per-session
# sink state that is not released at teardown. Reverting the backend to
# dbus-rel restored the station immediately, with the QEMU device and the
# new binary still in place -- so QEMU and the launcher are NOT at fault.
#
# TO COMPLETE THIS CUTOVER once the sink is fixed, flip ALL OF THESE TOGETHER.
# The list is exhaustive and was checked field by field against the reverted
# commit; flipping a subset leaves the station running correctly while DESCRIBING
# ITSELF WRONGLY, which is the failure class this whole wave was spent on.
#
#   this file        SH_INPUT_BACKEND  dbus-rel -> ramabs
#                    SH_CURSOR_SCALE   2.7778   -> 1.0
#   registry/stations/macos753.json
#                    stream.pointer                  (transport/backend/method/
#                                                     absolute/scale)
#                    spa.pointerRel                  true -> false
#                    operator.labctl.pointer_mode    rel  -> abs
#                    reset.pointer                   rel  -> abs
#                    runtime.qemu.emitArgs           --pointer/--input-backend/
#                                                    --cursor-scale
#                    operator.labctl.notes           <-- OPERATOR-FACING
#                    operator.actionMap.value.note   <-- OPERATOR-FACING
#                    runtime.qemu.deviceSetSummary   <-- must name the -chardev
#                                                        and -device kh-ramabs
#
# THE LAST THREE ARE THE EASY ONES TO MISS, and each states something that is
# false the moment the backend flips:
#   * the two note fields are what an operator READS; they currently describe
#     the relative ADB pointer and the 2.7778 scale, i.e. they would actively
#     mislead about a station that is no longer relative. `labctl.notes` is also
#     compared by the live-labctl gate check, so a half-flip shows up there.
#   * deviceSetSummary is the tracked description of the device set QEMU
#     actually starts. Both the tracked AND the live launcher already carry the
#     `-chardev socket,id=ptr0` / `-device kh-ramabs` pair, so a summary that
#     omits them is wrong TODAY -- it just does not block, because the current
#     method (qemu-adb-relative) requires no ledger token. At cutover the
#     `qemu-guestram-abswrite` method needs `kh-ramabs` in the ledger; it would
#     be satisfied via the launcher regardless, so this will NOT fail loudly.
#     Fix it because it is inaccurate, not because something will catch it.
#
# The QEMU binary and launcher are already installed and need no change: the
# device is armed with nothing connected to ptr.sock, which is inert while the
# daemon runs dbus-rel.
#
# ---- INSTALLED BINARY / LAUNCHER PAIRING - DO NOT SPLIT ----
# READ THIS FIRST IF QEMU REFUSES TO START ON THIS STATION.
# /opt/qemu-m68k/bin/qemu-system-m68k
# was REPLACED on 2026-08-30 with a build carrying the absolute-pointer
# patches, and the previous binary was kept beside it as
# <same path>.pre-abs-bak. The deployed launcher was updated in the same
# pass and now passes `-device kh-ramabs`, which the .pre-abs-bak binary
# DOES NOT SUPPORT (verified against the binaries, not the patch series).
# NOT YET RESTARTED onto this pair -- its running QEMU still holds the OLD
# inode. The next restart, for ANY reason, is the first exercise of it.
#
# THE PAIR IS BINDING IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. Restoring .pre-abs-bak while the
# current launcher stays installed makes QEMU refuse to start outright --
# kh-ramabs is an unknown device on that build. Rolling this station back to the
# old binary means restoring the OLD LAUNCHER TOO, in the same pass.
# Both new or both old. Never mixed.
#

# ---- CHECKPOINT FIXTURE (QEMU station, resetMode=loadvm) ----
# The scene is a quiet Mac OS 7.5.3 Finder desktop at 1152x870x8 — the
# authentic Apple 21-inch mode, and one of only TWO modes this machine can
# actually reach: macfb lists 640x480 and 800x600 for its VGA display type and
# 1152x870 for the Apple 21-inch type, but the VGA type always comes up 640x480
# no matter what -g says or how fresh the PRAM is, and Mac OS offers no
# resolution list at all (the declaration ROM exposes a single mode). So the
# geometry is a launcher decision, not a guest one, and changing it means
# re-baking the checkpoint.
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Quiet Mac OS 7.5.3 Finder desktop at 1152x870x8 (Quadra 800, 68040 under TCG): Macintosh HD top-right, empty Trash bottom-right, no window open, nothing selected. loadvm restores in 0.32 s, framebuffer-identical to the baked scene.

# ---- POINTER: RELATIVE ADB, and why the scale is not 1.0 ----
# This machine has no absolute pointer path of any kind — no USB bus, no
# tablet, no vmmouse — so the daemon runs its abs->rel bridge and the guest
# sees deltas only. Mac OS then applies its OWN units-to-pixels factor, and
# every tracking setting except the slowest is an ACCELERATION CURVE under
# which 1:1 is unreachable by construction. The checkpoint therefore pins the
# Mouse control panel to "Very Slow", which is dead linear at a measured
# 0.36 px per unit (1000 units -> 360 px, 1500 -> 540, 3000 -> 1079, and
# identical at every send chunk size from 1 to 32). SH_CURSOR_SCALE is that
# factor's reciprocal.
#
# Re-measure with:
#   scripts/install-vision/adb_pointer.py --qmp <CLONE>/qmp.sock gain
# against a CLONE. If the checkpoint is ever re-baked with a different tracking
# setting, this number is wrong and the pointer will drift proportionally.
SH_INPUT_BACKEND=dbus-rel
SH_CURSOR_SCALE=2.7778
# ---- RE-HOME BRIDGE (rel_bridge.rs; docs/lab/research/rel-pointer-rehome-and-rate-cap.md) ----
# macos753's ADB mouse QUEUES every count (nothing is ever lost), so the bridge
# NEVER DRIFTS once homed — measured 2026-08-18. It therefore needs re-homing on
# exactly one event: a `loadvm golden` restore, which teleports the guest cursor
# to the checkpoint position behind the model's back (reset-tile.sh -> SIGUSR2).
# The focus/idle/edge triggers and SH_REL_PACED are for the PS/2 *drift* stations;
# on this queuing ADB guest they only throw the cursor into the corner for the
# ~1 s an oversized pin (2048 px x 2.78 = 5688 units at 63/report) takes to drain.
SH_REL_HOME_ON=reset
# The guest cursor's baked position in the golden checkpoint (arrow-tip hotspot,
# measured from a screendump 2026-08-18: dead-centre of the 1152x870 desktop).
# On a reset the bridge seeds the model straight here — no corner pin, so the
# guest snaps under the visitor's pointer on their first move.
SH_REL_HOME_TO=599,500
# Raw sends — the QEMU adb-mouse button-barrier FIFO orders motion→button.

# ---- IDLE AUTO-PAUSE: ON from day one ----
# m68k runs under TCG — there is no KVM path for this architecture — so an idle
# station burns a real host core for nobody. It is a plain QEMU station, so the
# daemon's default QMP stop/cont path applies and needs no pidfile guard; the
# launcher additionally starts it PAUSED at the checkpoint (-loadvm golden -S),
# so a station nobody has visited has never run a single guest instruction.
# Warmup is 0 deliberately: there is no boot to protect, because the station
# never cold-boots in normal operation — it resumes an already-finished desktop.
# Rollback is one line: SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=0.
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=60
